 <section class="fasting-guide-section" id="fasting-imsak" aria-labelledby="fasting-imsak-heading">
  <h3 id="fasting-imsak-heading">When should you stop eating Suhoor: Imsak or Fajr?</h3>
  <p><strong>Suhoor</strong> is the pre-dawn meal. The daily fast begins at <strong>true dawn (Fajr)</strong>. The Quran describes eating and drinking until the dawn becomes discernible and then completing the fast until night. In <a href="https://sunnah.com/bukhari:7248" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sahih al-Bukhari 7248</a>, a report distinguishes the early-night call from the dawn call.</p>
  <p><strong>Imsak</strong> literally conveys restraint; in modern schedules it may be printed as a precautionary time 5, 10 or more minutes before Fajr. That early entry should not be mistaken for a separate universal obligatory beginning of the fast. Some communities apply additional safeguards because of uncertainty around dawn calculations. Follow your trusted local authority when their method differs from a generic app.</p>
  <div class="fasting-example">
   <h4>Example: a dawn timetable</h4>
   <div class="fasting-sample-times"><span>Optional reminder <strong>04:50</strong></span><span>Calculated Fajr <strong>05:00</strong></span><span>Calculated Maghrib <strong>18:30</strong></span></div>
   <p>These are hypothetical times, not today’s schedule for any city. An optional 10-minute reminder at 04:50 helps with planning but <strong>does not replace the Fajr calculation at 05:00</strong>. The actual religious start of fasting is tied to true dawn.</p>
  </div>
  <p>A calculation convention (such as Muslim World League, ISNA, Karachi or a locally used method) may change the estimated Fajr. Compare it with an authoritative mosque timetable. See <a href="/tools/prayer-times/">Prayer Times and calculation methods</a>.</p>
 </section>

 <section class="fasting-guide-section" id="fasting-iftar" aria-labelledby="fasting-iftar-heading">
  <h3 id="fasting-iftar-heading">When does Iftar start, and why is it tied to Maghrib?</h3>
  <p>Iftar is the breaking of the fast after sunset, corresponding to the start of Maghrib. <a href="https://sunnah.com/bukhari:1957" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sahih al-Bukhari 1957</a> encourages timely breaking of the fast once its ending has been established. There is no reason to automatically add an arbitrary delay to a verified sunset time.</p>
  <p>Maghrib is linked to local sunset, so it changes by date, location and horizon. A sunset time for another city is not automatically suitable for yours. The calculator’s iftar countdown is a convenience, not an independent sighting or confirmation that the sun has set at your exact horizon.</p>
  <p>A narration in <a href="https://sunnah.com/abudawud:2356" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sunan Abi Dawud 2356</a> describes breaking fast with fresh dates, dry dates or water. There is no need to postpone iftar until a full meal is ready.</p>
  <p>If you are travelling, at high altitude or in a place with unusual horizon conditions, ask your local authority how to apply the timetable. Travelling and exemptions from fasting also involve separate fiqh questions that this utility does not decide.</p>
 </section>

 <section class="fasting-guide-section" id="fasting-duas" aria-labelledby="fasting-duas-heading">
  <h3 id="fasting-duas-heading">Fasting duas and recommended remembrance</h3>
  <p>You can ask Allah sincerely in your own words around suhoor and iftar. There is <strong>no universal requirement to recite a fixed Arabic formula</strong> before every fast to make it valid. The following is a specifically narrated remembrance after breaking the fast, with a source and context.</p>
  <article class="fasting-dua-card">
   <h4>Dua reported when breaking the fast</h4>
   <p class="fasting-dua-arabic" lang="ar" dir="rtl">ذَهَبَ الظَّمَأُ وَابْتَلَّتِ الْعُرُوقُ وَثَبَتَ الْأَجْرُ إِنْ شَاءَ اللَّهُ</p>
   <p><strong>Transliteration:</strong> Dhahaba adh-dhama’u wabtallati al-‘uruqu wa thabata al-ajru in sha’a Allah.</p>
   <p><strong>Meaning:</strong> The thirst has gone, the veins have been moistened, and the reward is established, if Allah wills.</p>
   <p class="fasting-dua-reference">Reported in <a href="https://sunnah.com/abudawud:2357" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sunan Abi Dawud 2357</a> (graded Hasan by al-Albani as displayed by Sunnah.com). This is presented as a reported supplication, not as a condition for the validity of iftar.</p>
  </article>
  <div class="fasting-hadith-cards">
   <article><h4>Suhoor has blessing</h4><p>Even a modest pre-dawn meal can be meaningful. The Prophet ﷺ encouraged taking suhoor in <a href="https://sunnah.com/bukhari:1923" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sahih al-Bukhari 1923</a>. It is not compulsory in order for a fast to count.</p></article>
   <article><h4>Break the fast promptly</h4><p>Once sunset is established, timely iftar is encouraged in <a href="https://sunnah.com/bukhari:1957" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sahih al-Bukhari 1957</a>. Dates and water are described in <a href="https://sunnah.com/abudawud:2356" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sunan Abi Dawud 2356</a>.</p></article>
  </div>
  <p>If you encounter a widely circulated “Allahumma laka sumtu” formulation, check its exact source and grading rather than treating every familiar wording as equally established. The narrated remembrance above is linked so readers can review the report directly. For additional du'a topics, see <a href="https://www.islamic-relief.org.uk/resources/knowledge-base/dua/duas-for-fasting/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Islamic Relief UK's fasting dua guide</a>.</p>
 </section>

 <section class="fasting-guide-section" id="fasting-methods" aria-labelledby="fasting-methods-heading">
  <h3 id="fasting-methods-heading">Why fasting times differ: city, method and UK summer</h3>
  <p><strong>Fajr:</strong> Most astronomical prayer-time tools estimate true dawn from the Sun’s angle below the horizon. Different authorities use different angles and seasonal adjustments, so their Fajr results can differ even for the same city. The calculator lets you choose the method followed by your mosque.</p>
  <p><strong>Maghrib:</strong> It is tied to sunset and depends on latitude, longitude, elevation, atmospheric conditions and a local horizon. A computer-generated sunset is an estimate for a mathematical horizon, not an observation from your window.</p>
  <p><strong>UK high latitudes:</strong> During summer, especially in Scotland and northern England, the Sun may not reach standard twilight angles on some nights. Some authorities use alternative calculations or approximations for Fajr. The calculator offers high-latitude rules, but none is a universal binding religious answer. Follow the community timetable you trust.</p>
  <p><strong>Time zones and daylight saving:</strong> Choose the location’s correct IANA timezone (for example, Europe/London). Summer BST and winter GMT are handled by local-time formatting, so do not manually add an hour to an already displayed clock time.</p>
  <p><strong>Ramadan start:</strong> A fast-time calculator can show dawn and sunset on any civil date; it does not establish whether Ramadan has begun. Calendar decisions vary by moonsighting practice. Use the <a href="/tools/ramadan-timetable/">Ramadan timetable</a> only with a locally confirmed start date.</p>
  <p><strong>Not a religious or medical verdict:</strong> This guide does not decide exemptions, missed fasts, pregnancy, travel or illness. Seek appropriate qualified religious or healthcare advice where needed.</p>
 </section>

